WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.337
Now we combine several ideas into a more realistic cohort rule.

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:06.412
The patient must be active.

00:00:06.412 --> 00:00:09.102
Consent must be explicitly true.

00:00:09.102 --> 00:00:13.282
The diagnosis must be either Diabetes or Kidney Disease.

00:00:13.282 --> 00:00:17.767
Finally, the patient must have no usable email address.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:22.147
Notice that each requirement represents a different kind of logic.

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:25.147
Active status is a Boolean requirement.

00:00:25.147 --> 00:00:31.087
Consent uses explicit truth because NULL consent should not be treated as permission.

00:00:31.087 --> 00:00:34.517
Diagnosis uses membership in a target set.

00:00:34.517 --> 00:00:43.142
Email requires text normalization so NULL, empty text, and whitespace-only text all count as unusable.

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:48.352
All of these are connected with AND because every requirement must be satisfied.

00:00:48.352 --> 00:00:51.257
This resembles a real outreach workflow.

00:00:51.257 --> 00:00:58.642
Imagine a care-management team trying to identify people who need a phone call because they have a target

00:00:58.642 --> 00:01:03.802
chronic condition but cannot be reached by email.

00:01:03.802 --> 00:01:06.922
The important part is that the query is explainable.

00:01:06.922 --> 00:01:10.462
We can read it as a checklist.

00:01:10.462 --> 00:01:13.022
Active? Yes. Explicit consent? Yes.

00:01:13.022 --> 00:01:15.857
Target diagnosis? Yes. Usable email absent?

00:01:15.857 --> 00:01:18.432
Yes. Then include the patient.

00:01:18.432 --> 00:01:24.132
If Susan has the target diagnosis but consent is unknown, she should not qualify.

00:01:24.132 --> 00:01:30.382
If Robert has Kidney Disease, explicit consent, and no email, he may qualify.

00:01:30.382 --> 00:01:35.722
A strong query lets you explain each inclusion and exclusion in business language.
